{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 19, :comments ["<b>The first of the first fruits of your land.</b> The seventh-year produce is also obligated in first fruits — therefore it says here too בִּכּוּרֵי אַדְמָתְךָ. How so? A person enters his field, sees a fig that has ripened, ties a reed around it as a sign and designates it. And first fruits apply only to the seven species mentioned in Scripture — ״אֶרֶץ חִטָּה וּשְׂעֹרָה״ and so on (Bikkurim 1)." "<b>You shall not cook a kid.</b> A calf and a lamb are also included in גְּדִי, for גְּדִי is nothing but a term for a tender young animal — as you find in several places in the Torah where גְּדִי is written and it was necessary to specify after it עִזִּים, such as ״אָנֹכִי אֲשַׁלַּח גְּדִי עִזִּים״ (Genesis 38), ״אֶת גְּדִי הָעִזִּים״ (ibid.), ״שְׁנֵי גְּדָיֵי עִזִּים״ (ibid. 27) — to teach you that wherever גְּדִי is stated without specification, a calf and a lamb are also implied. And it is written in three places in the Torah: one for the prohibition of eating, one for the prohibition of deriving benefit, and one for the prohibition of cooking (Mekhilta; Ḥullin 115)."], :book :exodus, :voice :rashi, :lang :en, :chapter 23, :notes {}, :source-witnesses nil, :timestamp "2026-08-02T08:54:12.494252960Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}